5\" x2=\"285.6\" y2=\"52.17480044018865\" stroke=\"var(--fg)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"285.6\" y1=\"61.17480044018865\" x2=\"283.2706285940773\" y2=\"52.48146800358703\" stroke=\"var(--bad)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"285.6\" cy=\"61.17480044018865\" r=\"4.2\" fill=\"var(--bad)\" stroke=\"var(--surface)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"285.6\" y1=\"31.218542018015018\" x2=\"287.9293714059227\" y2=\"22.525209581413403\" stroke=\"var(--fg)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"285.6\" y1=\"31.218542018015018\" x2=\"283.2706285940773\" y2=\"22.525209581413403\" stroke=\"var(--bad)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"285.6\" cy=\"31.218542018015018\" r=\"4.2\" fill=\"var(--bad)\" stroke=\"var(--surface)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"48\" y1=\"322\" x2=\"66\" y2=\"322\" stroke=\"var(--bad)\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"70\" y=\"325\" font-size=\"8.5\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Lowest contrast correctly identified (descriptive; one presentation per level)</text><circle cx=\"54\" cy=\"334\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"var(--good)\"/><text x=\"61\" y=\"337\" font-size=\"8.5\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">identified</text><circle cx=\"114\" cy=\"334\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"var(--bad)\"/><text x=\"121\" y=\"337\" font-size=\"8.5\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">not identified</text><circle cx=\"194\" cy=\"334\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--muted)\"/><text x=\"201\" y=\"337\" font-size=\"8.5\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">not presented</text><circle cx=\"274\" cy=\"334\" r=\"5\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--warn)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"283\" y=\"337\" font-size=\"8.5\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">isolated</text></svg>","T":{"heading":"Visual contrast test results","banner":"Right eye 29 of 45 patches identified · Left eye 32 of 45 · Clinical significance not established","s1":"This test showed you 45 circular patches of faint parallel bars for the right eye and 45 for the left, at five bar widths (1.5, 3, 6, 12 and 18 cycles per degree) and nine contrast levels from 28.6 % down to 0.5 %. For each patch you chose whether the bars tilted left, stood upright or tilted right. The charts and tables record exactly what you were shown and what you answered. They describe how faint a pattern you identified; they do not identify a cause.","s2":"Screen size was set from a bank card (44.5 px/cm) and viewing distance entered as 50 cm, giving 38.8 pixels per degree. Brightness, colour settings and room lighting were not measured. The finest bars (18 cycles per degree) were drawn at 2.16 pixels per cycle. A sampled grating needs more than two pixels per cycle to be represented at all, and several more before its rendered contrast and shape approach the nominal values; at 2.16 pixels per cycle the column E patches were coarsely sampled and their effective contrast is uncertain. The three faintest contrast levels have, under the assumed rendering model (sRGB transfer function, 8-bit display, mid-grey background), computed amplitudes below one display step; what contrast those patches actually produced on this screen was not measured, and they are marked exploratory. Protocol: full (every patch presented). No change in browser zoom was detected during the test.","s4":"At each bar width the lowest contrast identified by the right eye and the left eye differed by 0.00 / 0.00 / -0.66 / -0.22 / 0.00 log units (a value above zero means the right eye identified a fainter patch). Differences of this size between a person's eyes can arise from the test's own variability; whether they are meaningful has not been established for this test.","s5":"This is a self-administered screen on an uncalibrated consumer display. Results depend on the screen, its settings, the room, attention and guessing (one in three answers would be right by chance). Each contrast level was shown once per eye, so a single lucky or unlucky answer moves the result. The test has not been validated against clinical contrast sensitivity instruments, and no normal range has been established for it. Reduced contrast sensitivity, where present, has many possible causes, including uncorrected refractive error, cataract, glaucoma, macular disease, diabetes, medication, alcohol and fatigue.","s6":"This test does not tell you whether your vision needs attention; an eye examination does. See an optometrist or your doctor if you have persistent difficulty seeing in dim light, fog, rain or glare, difficulty reading or recognising faces, any visual symptoms such as blur, haloes, distortion or a shadow in your vision, or any sudden change in your sight, which needs same-day attention. A consistent change across repeat tests taken under the same conditions is also worth discussing. Take this report with you; the numbers and charts are meant to be read alongside a clinical examination, not instead of one.","s7":"A contrast sensitivity result on its own does not identify mould, mycotoxin or any other environmental exposure, and cannot diagnose any illness. Research has reported group-level associations between reduced contrast sensitivity and exposure to water-damaged buildings; it has also reported that the handheld chart version does not separate exposed people from controls, and expert bodies have concluded that no validated test for such exposure exists. Questions about your home or workplace are answered by an environmental assessment, and questions about your health by a medical practitioner.","s8":"Why environmental toxin exposure matters to this test. Contrast sensitivity, the ability to see faint patterns, is one of the visual functions reported to change with exposure to a range of toxic agents. Published work has linked reduced contrast sensitivity at the middle spatial frequencies to living or working in water-damaged buildings [7, 8, 12], to exposure to toxins from cyanobacteria and dinoflagellates [4, 5, 6], and to solvents and other volatile organic compounds; contrast sensitivity at the finer bar widths is also affected by tobacco, alcohol, blood lead and age [11]. The same function changes with cataract, refractive error, glaucoma, macular disease, diabetes and medication [10, 11], which is why a result on its own cannot identify a cause [13, 14, 15]. MouldVision records your contrast function and, if you choose, the exposures and conditions you tell us about, so that changes over time can be read alongside what is happening in your environment. Testing the building itself (air and surface sampling) is the way to find out whether mould is present.","told":["Visible mould or a musty smell at home or work in the last 12 months: some","Water damage, flooding or persistent damp in the last 12 months: yes","Wearing your usual glasses or contacts now: yes","Age band: 50–59"],"toldHeading":"What you told us","s8Heading":"8. Environmental context","ident":"Run 252b87e2-c2b6-434d-bbda-accb29016f56 · 2026-10-09T11:02:26.135+11:00 · Software v0.7.0 (live) · Protocol full-5x9-v1 · Scoring score-v1-maxcorrect · Stimulus gauss-0.42R-sRGB-dither-v1 · Integrity SHA-256 050666a51c87 over test definition and responses (local integrity check, not proof of origin) · Geometry 44.5 px/cm (card), 50 cm (typed), 38.8 px/°, 2.16 px/cycle at 18 cpd, DPR 1 · Context context-v1-draft · Source hash unavailable","valid":"Software verification: not verified for this build (no test results). Physical measurement validation: not performed. Clinical validation: not performed; no diagnostic or exposure claim.","underRight":"29 of 45 presented patches identified; 1 answered can't see; 0 not presented; 0 isolated correct responses.","underLeft":"32 of 45 presented patches identified; 0 answered can't see; 0 not presented; 1 isolated correct responses.","storage":"Records are kept in this browser only. They are not permanent, not encrypted and not a clinical record. Export any record you want to keep.","chartCaption":"Lowest contrast correctly identified (descriptive; one presentation per level)","bandCaption":"Exploratory: computed sub-step amplitude under the assumed rendering model; displayed contrast not measured"},"sc":{"version":"score-v1-maxcorrect","right":{"presented":45,"correct":29,"incorrect":15,"cantSee":1,"notPresented":0,"scheduled":45,"lowestContrastCorrect":[200,120.6173,26.4575,15.9562,9.623],"lowestContrastCorrectRows1to6":[43.8702,43.8702,26.4575,15.9562,9.623],"isolated":[false,false,false,false,false],"byColumn":[{"col":"A","presented":9,"correct":9,"notPresented":0},{"col":"B","presented":9,"correct":8,"notPresented":0},{"col":"C","presented":9,"correct":5,"notPresented":0},{"col":"D","presented":9,"correct":4,"notPresented":0},{"col":"E","presented":9,"correct":3,"notPresented":0}]},"left":{"presented":45,"correct":32,"incorrect":13,"cantSee":0,"notPresented":0,"scheduled":45,"lowestContrastCorrect":[200,120.6173,120.6173,26.4575,9.623],"lowestContrastCorrectRows1to6":[43.8702,43.8702,43.8702,26.4575,9.623],"isolated":[false,false,true,false,false],"byColumn":[{"col":"A","presented":9,"correct":9,"notPresented":0},{"col":"B","presented":9,"correct":8,"notPresented":0},{"col":"C","presented":9,"correct":7,"notPresented":0},{"col":"D","presented":9,"correct":5,"notPresented":0},{"col":"E","presented":9,"correct":3,"notPresented":0}]},"interocularLog10":[0,0,-0.659,-0.22,0],"presented":90,"correct":61,"scheduled":90}}